<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">
  <!--
    The code both processes need.

    These three files were compiled into both projects by <Compile Include="..\Renderer\...">
    links in Main.fsproj, which meant Main reached into the renderer's source tree and, worse,
    that the two compilers wrote the same generated file. Whichever finished last decided its
    contents - and since a generated file imports Fable's library by a path relative to the
    project that compiled it, the main bundle ended up holding two copies of that library. Two
    copies of Map.js are two class hierarchies, so an F# Map built by one and read by the other
    answered for its root key and missed every other. That is what broke the context menus.

    The projects now reference this one, and each compiles to its own output directory (see
    scripts/dev.js), so no generated file has two authors.

    What belongs here: types and pure logic both processes need. Nothing that reaches the
    operating system - the renderer may not - and nothing that knows about the Elmish model,
    which only the renderer has. ContextMenus is the shape of that: the menu names and their
    items live here, while building an actual Electron menu is main's, in ContextMenuBuilder.fs.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <RuntimeIdentifiers>win-x64;linux-x64</RuntimeIdentifiers>
  </PropertyGroup>
  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- ElectronAPI first: ContextMenus uses its types. Log needs neither. -->
    <Compile Include="ElectronAPI.fs" />
    <Compile Include="Log.fs" />
    <Compile Include="ContextMenus.fs" />
  </ItemGroup>
  <PropertyGroup>
    <GenerateDocumentationFile>true</GenerateDocumentationFile>
  </PropertyGroup>
  <Import Project="..\..\.paket\Paket.Restore.targets" />
</Project>
